        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "When a laptop joins a network, it may know its hardware address but not its local IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, DNS servers, or lease policy. It cannot simply send a normal unicast request to a DHCP server because it does not yet know where that server is—or even which subnet it belongs to. DHCP bootstraps this uncertainty with a short exchange often remembered as DORA: Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledgment."
        },
        "model": "edge-tts-7.2.8",
        "parameters": {},
        "schemaVersion": 1
      },
